[Music]  this morning on the dish the man known  as the bread whisperer chef Chad  Robertson's culinary journey has taken  him across both the Atlantic and Pacific  Oceans he graduated from The Culinary  Institute of America and met his future  wife and business partner Elizabeth  along the way the couple worked and  trained throughout France and then  returned to the US where they opened San  Francisco's popular Tartine bakery and  just last month Tartine opened its first  overseas flagship with a location in  South Korea and another bakery in the  works for Los Angeles chef Chad  Robertson good morning welcome to the  dish thank you pleasure to be here we've  got a spectacular table tell us what's  here well yeah we haven't quite an  assortment this is ricotta and cornmeal  upside-down cake with a winter citrus on  top you kind of have a theme with a lot  of these things in the dishes we have a  little gem salad with shaved carrots and  also ricotta on the bottom with some  fresh herbs  porchetta and the center there mmm mmm  and over here shelling beans in broth  and then we have at our team which is  the toasted bread with also ricotta and  winter citrus amazing all right I don't  see West Texas in its table but that's  where you're from yeah so tell us a  little bit about how that shaped the man  yeah well so I grew up in West Texas and  you know pretty simple food for the most  part lots of good barbecue we all know  but my my mom and dad were both pretty  adventurous cooks in different ways so I  was definitely open to new ideas and  sort of enjoying seeing things that I  hadn't seen before your dad was actually  a punter for the New England Patriots it  was when you were a ranked tennis player  as a teenager I was yeah sports are a  big part of West Texas and all of Texas  but the interesting thing about my dad  is of course I had to play a sport but  he didn't really care what as long as  you know I really focused on something  so I tried everything and I settled on  tennis but it's not we're doing  professionally your grandfather was a  craftsman I read that that sort of  working with your hands was a big part  of the war of of cooking my grandfather  on my mother's side and  great-grandfather on the same side our  cowboy boots saddle makers super  traditional stuff and I kind of grew up  in the boot shop for a few years and  just seeing people work with their hands  you know it was definitely drawn to that  I didn't know what I wanted to do  exactly I wanted to travel so the idea  of learning how to cook sort of seemed  to hit both of those boxes where I could  travel anywhere  work with my hands and find work you  know anywhere in the world so at the  culinary institute you not only learned  about cooking but but from what I gather  alphabetical proximity is what  ultimately led you to your story your  wife yeah yeah that was a funny thing  because there were there were mostly  guys in the class and like maybe two or  three girls and and my lovely wife was  one of them and our names were together  in alphabetical order so we were put  together as partners mm-hmm so we  started as friends and then we started  dating and you know it's been 25 years  so then you've traveled the world  together learning baking techniques and  learning more about bread which is  really the showstopper at Tartine you  guys are using ancient grains why is  that important and what do they lend the  bread that other yeah so I took a trip  to Denmark and that was where the whole  idea of of ancient grains came from  because Klaus Meyer had been working on  this with the whole team of breeders and  and farmers for years and they they  brought all these ancient Nordic grains  back and and all the chefs in Denmark  were also this is six or seven years ago  still but doing really interesting  things and it just dawned on me that you  know as a baker I grew up with white  flour and whole wheat flour and that's  it you know and really you know dozens  of hundreds of different grains  now people reserve your bread in advance  that happens yeah we're making more  bread now for so long you know 15 years  we only had a certain capacity and now  that we have another spot we make more  bread but we still make a point of  baking throughout the day so you can  always get a hot loaf of bread if you  come to our team and you guys have  expanded to Korea world domination for a  Tartine curry has been fun yeah chef as  they ask you to sign this dish I will  ask you the question we ask everyone on  this part of the program if  you could share this meal with any  figure pastor present who would it be  you know honestly with all the travel  and all the work and all the expansion  when I'm thinking about who I want to  share a meal with right now it's it's  with my wife and my daughter because I  don't have enough of those right on  right on chef chef Chad Robertson thank  you and congratulations on all your  success and for more on Chad Robertson  and the dish head to our website at CBS  this morning dot-com

